    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How do its dimensions compare to a cylindrical can with the same volume and a minimum surface area?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What other considerations do manufacturers have when deciding on the dimensions of the cans, besides minimizing the amount of material used?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Some relationships cannot be described by polynomial functions. For example, let’s think about the relationship between the radius \\(r\\), in centimeters, and the surface area \\(S\\), in square centimeters, of the set of cylinders with a…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We know these formulas are true for all cylinders with radius \\(r\\), height \\(h\\), surface area \\(S\\), and volume \\(V\\):",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle S=2\\pi r^2 +2\\pi rh\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle V=\\pi r^2h\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Since we are only interested in cylinders with a volume of 330 cm3, we can use the volume formula to rewrite the surface area formula as:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle S=2\\pi r^2+\\frac{660}{r}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Can you see how? By using the volume formula rearranged as \\(h=\\frac{330}{\\pi r^2}\\) and then substituting \\(\\frac{330}{\\pi r^2}\\) for \\(h\\) in the formula for surface area.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We now have an equation giving \\(S\\) as a function of \\(r\\) for cylinders with a volume of 330 cm3. From the graph of \\(S\\) shown here, we can quickly identify that a radius of about 3.75 cm results in a cylinder with minimum surface area…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(S\\) is an example of a rational function. Rational functions are fractions with polynomials in the numerator and denominator. Polynomial functions are a type of rational function with 1 in the denominator.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "In this situation, the height of a cylinder with fixed volume varies inversely with the square of the radius, \\(h=\\frac{330}{\\pi r^2}\\), which means that as the value of \\(r^2\\) increases, the value of the height decreases, and vice versa.…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L16-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Calculating Surface Area",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A rational function is a function defined by a fraction with polynomials in the numerator and denominator. Rational functions include polynomials because a polynomial can be written as a fraction with denominator 1.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
